markdown <<MARKDOWN
# What Is Machine Learning?

<img src="https://images.code.org/f23c55f4bc6d89b83cdd6cfe5f91e422-image-1793739001885.png" alt="doodle of cat" width="120px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0px 30px 0px 30px;">

Lesson 1 opens with a hook: "Cat or Dog?" Students vote on a series of doodles, cat or dog, one at a time. The last one is deliberately ambiguous, built to spark real debate.

Then comes the real pivot: "Looking at these doodles, what rules, if any, could we use to distinguish between cats and dogs?" Students try: "cats have whiskers," "dogs have floppy ears," and the lesson is built to immediately surface counterexamples, a dog with whiskers, a cat with pointy ears. The point isn't to fail, it's to feel how hard this problem actually is to solve with explicit rules.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>


<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> this is a direct callback to Unit 1's "Limits of Logic-Based AI." Students already learned some problems are too hard to solve with hand-written rules, this lesson shows them one concretely, then offers a different approach entirely.</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128214; Machine learning:</strong> a field of AI focused on how computers can learn how to do something instead of being told how to do it.</div>
</div>

Four more terms build out the rest of the picture, click each card below to see its definition:

<div style="width:100%; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden; margin:20px 0;">
  <iframe
    src="https://amandadennard-dotcom.github.io/AIFY2/unit-2/ml-vocab-flip-cards.html"
    width="100%"
    height="500"
    frameborder="0"
    scrolling="no"
    title="Machine learning vocabulary flip cards: data, model, training, training data">
  </iframe>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> the lesson's own three-step process is worth repeating verbatim to students:
    <ol style="margin: 10px 0 10px 20px; padding-left: 10px;">
      <li style="margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Gather data</strong></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Train a model</strong> with the data</li>
      <li><strong>Use the model</strong> to make predictions about new data</li>
    </ol>
    Students apply this exact sequence in the Doodle Identifier activity (Lesson 1, Levels 1-2), training it to recognize their own drawings.
  </div>
</div>




